      "code_sha256_lf": "a35a62194202aa4766f5d03d260488ba603f66de01edfe4b357ed986668aafc6",
      "rationale": "The reported regression comes from an off-by-one in TokenList.group_tokens' extend=True branch (sqlparse/sql.py: `self.tokens[start_idx + 2:end_idx]` instead of `start_idx + 1`), which is exercised every time group_identifier_list() extends an already-built IdentifierList for a second (or later) comma. This silently removes the triggering comma token from the tree, so a plain parse/str round trip of a 3+ column select loses a comma - something sqlparse's own documentation (docs/source/intro.rst) guarantees never happens, since the parser is explicitly \"non-validating\" and str(stmt) must reproduce the input exactly when no formatting options are used. I verified by tracing group_identifier_list/_group/group_tokens step by step for \"select a, b, c from tbl\" that the second merge sets start=the existing IdentifierList (isinstance check true), so the buggy slice drops the second comma, giving \"select a, b c from tbl\"; the equivalent 4-column case and the plain sqlparse.format() no-op path fail the same way today and will pass once the slice is corrected to start_idx + 1, without affecting any already-correct behavior.",
